\section*{Project 6 -- Multiple time scale solutions of models for a pendulum}

This project is concerned with ODEs which describe the swing of a
pendulum. You will construct an approximate analytic solution of these
ODEs by using asymptotic/perturbation methods which exploit the presence
of a small, dimensionless parameter
\[
0 < \epsilon < 1.
\]

\subsection*{Part 1}

Consider the weakly nonlinear Duffing equation
\begin{equation}
    y'' + y + \epsilon y^3 = 0,
    \tag{12}
\end{equation}
where
\[
    {}' \equiv \frac{d}{dt},
\]
with initial data
\[
    y(0)=1,
    \qquad
    y'(0)=0.
\]

\begin{enumerate}
    \item[(i)]
    Use the regular perturbation series
    \[
        y \sim y_0(t) + \epsilon y_1(t)
    \]
    to show that
    \begin{equation}
        y \sim \cos(t)
        + \epsilon
        \left(
        \alpha\cos(3t)
        - \alpha\cos(t)
        - \frac{3}{8}t\sin(t)
        \right),
        \tag{13}
    \end{equation}
    where $\alpha$ is a constant you should determine.

    State why the expansion from above is not valid at
    \[
        t=\mathcal{O}(\epsilon^{-1}).
    \]

    \item[(ii)]
    Use the method of multiple-scales to obtain the leading order
    approximation
    \begin{equation}
        y(t)
        =
        \cos\left[(1+k\epsilon)t\right]
        +\mathcal{O}(\epsilon),
        \tag{14}
    \end{equation}
    where $k$ is a constant to be found.

    Discuss why this expansion is better than the one from (13), in
    terms of its validity at large time.

    Use (14) to determine an approximation for the period of $y$.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Part 2}

Consider the equation for a pendulum
\begin{equation}
    \theta'' + \sin(\theta)=0,
    \tag{15}
\end{equation}
with initial data
\[
    \theta(0)=\epsilon,
    \qquad
    \theta'(0)=0.
\]

\begin{enumerate}
    \item[(i)]
    When considering finding an approximate solution to this ODE using
    multiple scale analysis, state why it is appropriate to introduce
    the new timescale variables and perturbation series
    \begin{equation}
        t_0=t,
        \qquad
        t_1=\epsilon^2t,
        \qquad
        \theta
        \sim
        \epsilon\theta_0(t_0,t_1)
        +\epsilon^n\theta_1(t_0,t_1),
        \tag{16}
    \end{equation}
    where $n>0$ is a constant you should determine.

    \item[(ii)]
    Show that
    \begin{equation}
        \theta_0
        =
        A(t_1)\cos(t_0)
        +
        B(t_1)\sin(t_0),
        \qquad
        A(0)=1,
        \qquad
        B(0)=0,
        \tag{17}
    \end{equation}
    where $A$ and $B$ are functions of integration that need not be
    determined at this stage.

    \item[(iii)]
    By considering the system governing the evolution of $\theta_1$,
    show that
    \begin{equation}
        A=\cos\left(\frac{t_1}{16}\right),
        \qquad
        B=\sin\left(\frac{t_1}{16}\right).
        \tag{18}
    \end{equation}

    Hence, determine the temporal interval of validity of the first
    order term of $\theta$. Determine an approximation for the period
    of $\theta$.
\end{enumerate}

